ounded_unit_claim": true}}], "viewerUrl": "https://us.cloud.langfuse.com/project/cmmxymjnc032zad07j35swt3h/traces/91087a1def7a97175462de54f8668c86", "ts": "2026-09-28T09:41:25.927Z"}, "id": 3, "domain": "VFD / Variable Frequency Drive Faults & Operation", "difficulty": "medium", "type": "scenario", "correct_answer": "A", "model_answer": "A", "parse_rule": "letter_on_own_line", "is_correct": true, "http": 200, "turn_status": "answered", "basis": "general_reasoning", "trace_id": "91087a1def7a97175462de54f8668c86", "response_time_ms": 9253, "answer_text": "⚠️ **Safety flag on a step below:** “- Check the drive input voltage – with the drive locked out and verified de-energized, measure the line voltage at the drive's input terminals while the unit is”. Do not do that on energized, pressurized or moving equipment — isolate it and verify zero energy first.\n\nA  \n\n- **Check the drive input voltage** – with the drive locked out and verified de‑energized, measure the line voltage at the drive’s input terminals while the unit is powered; a low or missing input will directly limit the output voltage.  \n- Verify that the input voltage is within the drive’s rated range (typically ±10 % of nameplate).  \n- If input is correct, confirm the motor name‑plate voltage matches the drive’s output rating (ensure the motor is the correct 460 V type).  \n- Next, check the drive’s output current limit setting to see if it’s limiting voltage under load.  \n- Finally, review the carrier frequency setting only if the above are all normal.  \n\n(All checks must be done with proper lock‑out/tag‑out and qualified personnel for any energized work.)"}
