}], "viewerUrl": "https://us.cloud.langfuse.com/project/cmmxymjnc032zad07j35swt3h/traces/0607f6a071234457c1155b6c8601466c", "ts": "2026-09-28T09:41:34.526Z"}, "id": 6, "domain": "VFD / Variable Frequency Drive Faults & Operation", "difficulty": "medium", "type": "scenario", "correct_answer": "A", "model_answer": "A", "parse_rule": "leading_letter", "is_correct": true, "http": 200, "turn_status": "answered", "basis": "general_reasoning", "trace_id": "0607f6a071234457c1155b6c8601466c", "response_time_ms": 4988, "answer_text": "A\n\n- First, confirm the drive’s ambient temperature rating in the manual; if the panel is hotter than the rated 40 °C, the drive must be derated.  \n- 1️⃣ Verify the actual panel temperature with a calibrated thermometer; ensure the reading is stable at 45 °C.  \n- 2️⃣ Consult the drive’s derating chart (usually a % reduction per °C above rating) and calculate the allowable current limit.  \n- 3️⃣ Program the VFD to the reduced current limit and monitor the motor current to stay within that limit.  \n- 4️⃣ If possible, improve panel cooling (add ventilation or a fan) to bring ambient back within spec.  \n\nDo you have access to the drive’s derating table so we can compute the exact current reduction?"}
